::GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What is the hypothesis in: If a figure is a square, then it has four congruent sides?
{
=A. A figure is a square.#Correct. The hypothesis is the if part.
~B. It has four congruent sides.#This is the conclusion.
~C. It is a rectangle.#This is not the stated hypothesis.
~D. The sides are parallel.#This may be related but is not the if part.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What is the converse of: If two angles are vertical, then they are congruent?
{
~A. If two angles are not vertical, then they are not congruent.#This is the inverse.
=B. If two angles are congruent, then they are vertical.#Correct. The converse switches hypothesis and conclusion.
~C. If two angles are not congruent, then they are not vertical.#This is the contrapositive.
~D. If two angles are vertical, then they are not congruent.#This changes the conclusion incorrectly.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
Which statement has the same truth value as the original conditional?
{
~A. Converse#The converse must be checked separately.
~B. Inverse#The inverse is not always equivalent to the original.
=C. Contrapositive#Correct. The contrapositive is logically equivalent.
~D. Biconditional#A biconditional requires both directions to be true.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q04::
Question ID: GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
When is a biconditional valid?
{
~A. When only the conditional is true.#One direction is not enough.
~B. When only the inverse is true.#The inverse does not establish a biconditional.
~C. When no hypothesis exists.#Biconditionals still connect two statements.
=D. When the conditional and converse are both true.#Correct. Biconditionals work in both directions.
}

::GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q05::
Question ID: GEO_U02_L02_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.RP.4
Question:
What is the inverse of: If a polygon is a triangle, then it has three sides?
{
=A. If a polygon is not a triangle, then it does not have three sides.#Correct. The inverse negates both parts without switching.
~B. If a polygon has three sides, then it is a triangle.#This is the converse.
~C. If a polygon does not have three sides, then it is not a triangle.#This is the contrapositive.
~D. If a polygon is a triangle, then it does not have three sides.#This only changes the conclusion.
}

